  "query_preview": "..The remnants of Tropical Storm Rachel continue to lift \nnorth, while a strong upper level low digs down through the Great \nBasin. Showers and thunderstorms continue to move northward through \ncentral Arizona overnight, with additional storms expected through \nthis afternoon. \n\nModel soundings show PWATs dropping off quite a bit from previous \ndays but still remaining near or above the 75 percentile. Heavy rain \nwill still be a threat but with dry air pushing in aloft and \nmoisture being largely confined to the lower levels, it may not be \nas widespread as it was over the weekend. Hi-res models still show \nthe potential for heavy rain across western Arizona, mainly Yavapai \nand Coconino counties through this morning, with areas across the \nhigher terrain continuing to see potential for showers and \nthunderstorms through this afternoon and early evening. With the low \ncrossing the region on Tuesday, we will likely continue to see \nscattered showers and thunderstorms continue, especially across the \nhigher terrain, through Tuesday evening, tapering off Tuesday night \ninto early Wednesday. \n\nRainfall amounts across the region over the past couple of days have \nranged between 1-3\", with a few locally higher amounts. While \ncoverage of storms may end up being more scattered than numerous \nthrough this afternoon, the wet antecedent conditions mean any storm \nwith decent rainfall rates could lead to flooding. With that in \nmind, the Flood Watch has been extended and remains in effect \nthrough 5 PM for areas along and south of the Mogollon Rim up \nthrough the Kaibab Plateau.\n\nWith the low digging down, we don't see the pressure gradient \nrelax all that much, which will leave us with continued gusty \nwinds through the day. Strongest winds will be downwind of the \nhigher terrain and through the Little Colorado River Valley, where \ngusts to 40-50 mph will be possible. Coverage of the higher winds \nlooks to be more limited than they were on Sunday and therefore no \nWind Advisory has been issued. \n\nThe low slowly moves to the east Wednesday before becoming cut \noff from the main jet and settling over the southwest through the \nend of the week. Dry air will get pulled into the region and we \nshould see chances for showers and thunderstorms dwindle with \ndry conditions expected through Thursday. The low slides to the \nsouthwest and moisture starts to creep back in on the eastern \nedge, which may allow for showers to return for Friday through the \nweekend. Expect cooler temperatures to continue with gusty northeast \nto east winds.",
